  "full_prompt": "Create an animation explaining integrals and the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus. Start with the car speedometer problem: given velocity v(t) = t(8-t), find the distance traveled. Show that velocity is constantly changing, so approximate by dividing time into small intervals dt and computing v(t)·dt for each, summing them. Show that as dt→0, the sum approaches the area under the velocity curve. Introduce the integral symbol ∫ as a stretched 'S' for sum. Then show that the area function A(T) has derivative A'(T) = v(T), because a small nudge dT adds a sliver of area v(T)·dT. Thus, to compute the integral, find an antiderivative F such that F' = v, then evaluate F(b) - F(a). This is the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus. Also show the concept of signed area (negative area when graph goes below axis).",
